            <ArticleTitle>Recurrent ectopic pregnancy after ipsilateral partial salpingectomy: a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>540-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Ectopic pregnancy is associated with maternal morbidity and mortality during early pregnancy. Ectopic pregnancy occurs in approximately 2% of all pregnancies, and the risk of ectopic pregnancy is increased by eight-fold in women with a history of eopic pregnancy. However, recurrent ectopic pregnancy after ipsilateral partial salpingectomy is quite rare. The authors experienced a case of recurrent ectopic pregnancy in the distal remnant after right partial salpingectomy. In this case report, they discuss this unusual case and provide a brief review of the literature.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
